  "review_text": "[SPAN: display=block; fontsize=extralarge; fonttype=bold; text=1963 End of the Year Review]\nNew to the awards?&nbsp; See the [PAGE: awards-index] for full descriptions.\n\n[DIVIDER]\n1963 brought us 78 new books -- not counting the [PAGE: meanwhile] --  containing 88 actually reviewed and graded stories.&nbsp; The output nearly doubled in terms of actually-published issues from 1962 to 1963 (38 to 78) and <i>more</i> than doubled in terms of stories (41 to 88)<i>.</i>\n\nIn the end, the final weighted average was [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=large; fonttype=bold; text=x.xx], which included 23 stories that were at least mid-grade (6.0 or better), though 45 (nearly double) were <i>below</i> readable (less than 4.0), so the net result was that, while stories <i>could</i> occasionally be good, readers were forced to sift through a lot of baggage to find them.\n\nFurthermore, there were precious few serviceable, middle-of-the-road stories to be found, meaning that the normal experience tended to be stories that were either solid (at times, even excellent)... or poor (at times, even abysmal).&nbsp; And sadly, the bad more than outweighed the good in any given month.\n\nThe uneven reading experience undoubtedly left readers still clinging to the promise of something great in the works, without the consistency needed to convince them that Marvel held any long-term alternative to more <i>Dominant Competitors</i>.\n\n\nSome highlights/lowlights of the year (in no particular order):\ndebut of major characters Doctor Strange, Wasp, Avengers and X-Men!\n(Giant-Man also debuts, but it's just a rebranded Ant-Man.)\n\nmany villains and supporting characters also debut, including the Watcher, Vulture, Mad Thinker, Doc Ock, Sandman, Baron Mordo, Betty Brant, Super-Skrull, Pepper Potts, Happy Hogan, Lady Dorma, Warlord Krang, Porcupine, Magneto, Rama Tut, Molecule Man, Crimson Dynamo, Lizard, Melter, Cobra, Mister Hyde, Vanisher, the Blob, Electro, Mandarin, the Enforcers, to name a few.\nIt seems that Dr. Doom and the Sub-Mariner were lurking around every corner.&nbsp; And even though I'm not reviewing <i>Sgt. Fury</i>, Nick Fury is still a significant ongoing character in the Marvel Universe.\nthe <i>Incredible Hulk</i> was canceled (which came as no surprise since it was downright dreadful)\n<i>Strange Tales</i> becomes the first book to feature co-stars, with the Human Torch and Doctor Strange having to share the title.&nbsp; Since Marvel was limited in the number of books it was allowed to distribute every month, more will soon follow....\nThe <i>Tales of Asgard</i> feature in <i>Journey Into Mystery</i> debuts and seems to be a solid success.\n<i>Tales of the Watcher</i> back-up in TOS reads better <i>The Wasp Tells a Tale</i> in TTA.&nbsp; The Watcher's role is put to far better use.\nThe Captain America try-out in <i>Strange Tales </i>#114 leads to the decision to bring back the character for good in 1964.\nThe Hulk quitting the Avengers in only the second issue likely comes as a complete surprise to all readers out there.\nIron Man's appearance changes a couple of times in 1963, finally settling closer (but not quite getting) to the longtime look he eventually gets the next year.\nPeter Parker decks Flash Thompson in the boxing ring - <i>surely</i> a top moment of 1963!\nSpider-Man's supporting cast is clearly the most developed of all the mags, <i>far</i> superior than Jane Foster, Alicia Masters, Rick Jones, Pepper Potts and Harry Hogan (and just who else is there besides these anyway?).&nbsp; The X-Men's last issue of the year hinted that their personalities might be worth paying attention to, but then again, they're the <i>main</i> characters and not the <i>supporting</i> ones.&nbsp; The wealth that's available around Peter Parker is immense, and issues 8 and 10 in particular drive home how his full cast can play a nice role in the crafting of good stories.\nSue's new invisible force field power is a welcome addition and finally makes her equally as formidable as the other three have always been.\nBen's first outcries of \"<i>It's Clobberin' Time!</i>\" feels like welcoming in an old friend to the mix.\nDoctor Strange has done nothing yet to make me look forward to reading his offering of the month....(and well, neither has Ant-Man/Giant-Man).\nMarvel has multiple crossover stories involving Spider-Man and the Human Torch, the FF with both Ant-Man and Nick Fury, Iron Man and the Angel.&nbsp; The shared Marvel universe is gradually getting featured!\nthe early issues of <i>Amazing Spider-Man</i> really <i>do</i> stand out in terms of quality.&nbsp; A little drop off near the end of the year, but still mostly solid.&nbsp; It becomes so much of an instant success that it quickly shifts to a monthly release schedule.\nTop issues of the year include <i>Amazing Spider-Man</i> #3, 4 and 10.&nbsp; All well done!\nWorst story of the year:\no    [LINK:1963:HULK_6|HULK #6|2026-07-07]\no    [LINK:1963:TOS_41_iron0|TOS #41|2026-08-12] - the <i>first</i> Dr. Strange!&nbsp; (which I note could also have taken the Spiral Award since we go from villain introduction to cheers from \"power-mad military men\" from all over the world over the course of three story pages!&nbsp; <i>Holy cow!</i>\no    With <i>Avengers</i> #2 making a strong showing in this regard as well - <i>yeesh</i>!\nBest back-up story of the year: \"One of Us Must Die\" from [LINK:1963:TOS_44_iron0|TOS #44|2026-10-14], with at least a shout-out to \"Shock!\" from [LINK:1963:TOS_47_iron0|TOS #47|2027-01-02] and a nod to \"No Place to Turn!\" from [LINK:1963:TTA_51_giant0|TTA #51|2027-02-09] as well.\nBiggest belly laugh of the year goes to the back-up tale \"Target...Earth!\" from [LINK:1963:TTA_47_ant0|TTA #47|2026-10-23], <i>heh</i>.&nbsp; (Though I wouldn't blame anyone for laughing out loud at the face of the Tinkerer in the second story that made up [LINK:1963:ASM_2_asm1|ASM #2|2026-07-31] - what a creep!)\nBest Panel of the Year\no    [LINK:1963:FF_14|FF #14|2026-08-06], bottom of page 6 - the Thing has had enough of the Sub-Mariner's deep sea fungus (and apparently of Namor's endless chatter)!\no    [LINK:1963:ASM_3|ASM #3|2026-09-11] - top-right of page 12\nSpiral Award finalists:\no    [LINK:1963:JIM_91_thor0|JIM #91|2026-07-25]:&nbsp; Sandu goes from \"Wow, look, I can levitate things!\" to \"I can rule the world!\" over the course of three pages, all while Loki pulls the strings from up in Asgard.&nbsp; (But hey, it's the Silver Age - there's no time to waste!)\nStill <i>way</i> too many stories involving \"I am your master.&nbsp; You are my slave.\" and alien invasions from other galaxies, all lining up to conquer Earth!\nBottom line:&nbsp; while a significant chunk of the books remained at or above reader level, and there were indeed some stand-out issues, let's be honest here.&nbsp; This was <i>not</i> a great year in terms of consistent quality, with the May issues being the worst Marvel has ever published to date (and I'll be surprised if they ever descend to that abysmal level again).&nbsp; There were 3 or 4 months in there where the outlook appeared very grim indeed.\nSo far, still at an early juncture in its history, Marvel was also being respectful of its competition, as you can read in some of the letters pages, encouraging its fans to read above all else.\nHowever, all I can conclude is that the competitors must have been publishing some truly dreadful offerings for <i>this</i> batch of Marvels to appear better.&nbsp; It was a rougher year than 1962, but I sense that there is a lot of promise still to come and look forward to the next year!\n\n\n<b>Top Covers</b>\n\n<b>FIXME</b>\n\n<b>Best Panel of the Year</b>\n\n[LINK:1963:FF_14|FF #14|2026-08-06]&nbsp;     bottom of page 16 -- the Thing has had <i>enough</i> of the Sub-Mariner's deep sea fungus (and apparently of Namor's endless chatter)!\n[ASSET: center | ThingHasHadEnough | width=50%]\n[LINK:1963:ASM_3|ASM #3|2026-09-11]&nbsp;     front lit Doctor Octopus\n[ASSET: center | DocOckFrontLit | width=50%]\n[LINK:1963:ASM_4|ASM #4|2026-10-26]&nbsp;     Spider-Man bursts in on the Sandman\n[ASSET: center | SpiderManBurstsIn | width=50%]\n[LINK:1963:ASM_8|ASM #8|2027-02-12]&nbsp;     Flash Thompson gets brained by \"puny\" Peter Parker in the boxing ring\n[ASSET: center | FlashBrained | width=50%]\n\nWhat about that Thor panel?????????????????????\n\n<b>Best Story of the Year</b>\n\n<b>FIXME</b> - several ASM stories come to mind....\n\n\n<b>Worst Story of the Year</b>\n\n[HANG0] [LINK:1963:HULK_6|HULK #6|2026-07-07] - the ill-fated Metal Master showed us that no matter how bad the <i>previous </i>issues of the Hulk magazine had been, [SPAN: severity=error; text=we hadn't seen <i>nothing</i> yet!]\n[HANG0] [LINK:1963:TOS_41_iron0|TOS #41|2026-08-12] -- the <i>first</i> Dr. Strange!&nbsp; (which I note could also have taken the Spiral Award since we go from villain introduction to cheers from \"power-mad military men\" from all over the world over the course of three story pages!&nbsp; Thank goodness there was a flashlight nearby!&nbsp; <i>Holy cow!</i>\n[HANG0] [LINK:1963:AVEN_2|AVEN #2|2027-01-08] -- the Space Phantom debuts in a tale riddled with so many plot holes to make one's head spin -- <i>yeesh</i>!\n[HANG0] [LINK:1963:ST_117_torch1|ST #117torch|2027-03-19] -- While neither of the two stories got rock-bottom marks, it was the brutal <i>pair</i> of 1.8 ratings that left readers with a bad taste in their mouths.\n[HANG0] [LINK:1963:FF_24|FF #24|2027-04-08] -- The Infant Terrible?&nbsp; I don't think so!&nbsp; <b>Issue Terrible</b> was more like it!\n\nFrom the candidates <i>above</i> however, I give the nod to:&nbsp; xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx\n\n\n<b>Biggest Blunder</b>\n\n[LINK:1963:ASM_3|ASM #3|2026-09-11]&nbsp;     Doc Ock calls Spider-Man \"Super-Man\"\n[ASSET: center | DocOckSuperMan | width=50%]\n[LINK:1963:AVEN_3|AVEN #3|2027-03-01]&nbsp;   Bruce Banner transforms, not only into the Hulk, but also to \"Bob\"\n[ASSET: center | BobBanner | width=50%]\n\n<b>Best Back-Up Story</b>\n[BR] There were actually some pretty good back-up tales to be found buried amidst this year's batch of books.\n\n[HANG0] [LINK:1963:TOS_44_iron0|TOS #44|2026-10-14] -- \"One of Us Must Die\"\n[HANG0] [LINK:1963:TOS_47_iron0|TOS #47|2027-01-02] -- \"Shock!\"\n[HANG0] [LINK:1963:TTA_51_giant0|TTA #51|2027-02-09] -- \"No Place to Turn!\"\n\nBut none of these held a candle to [LINK:1963:TTA_47_ant0|TTA #47|2026-10-23]'s \"Target... Earth!\"&nbsp; However, since that one was primarily best for its laugh-out-loud moment, see Best Belly-Laugh Moment award elsewhere.\n\nFrom the candidates <i>above</i> however, I give the nod to:&nbsp; xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx\n\n<b>Best Belly-Laugh Moment</b>\n\nThere were some funny moments this year:\n\n[ASSET: right | JamesonVulture | width=12%] [LINK:1963:ASM_7|ASM #7|2027-01-20] -- The sight of J. Jonah Jameson arguing with the Vulture, trying to convince him <i>not</i> to rob his payroll, <i>heh</i>.\n\n[ASSET: right | SamsDelicatessen | width=12%] [LINK:1963:FF_22|FF #22|2027-02-17] -- Reed's look of exacerbation on page 7, panel 3, having to explain to a \"Mrs. Frobush\" on the phone that she has the wrong number, that \"This <i>isn't</i> Sam's Delicatessen!\"\n\n[DEVNOTE: Target...Earth!&nbsp; (put here for searching purposes)]\n\nThis award actually goes to the <b>backup</b> tale in [LINK:1963:TTA_47_ant0|TTA #47|2026-10-23] called \"Target... Earth!\" (by Stan Lee and Steve Ditko).\n\nVery much in line with one of the 1961's Trope Trifecta, this one detailed Ba-Korr and the warriors of the planet Klang, who are looking for a new world to conquer, and lo and behold they find \"a weak planet named Earth! (<i>Oh no!</i>)&nbsp; \"in a far-off galaxy\" no less!&nbsp; Ba-Korr sends Sagar, his mightiest warrior to scout Earth's defenses... and get <i>this</i> -- if he doesn't report back within 48 hours, the invasion will be called off!\n\nAnd so, with only 48 hours to do his scouting job, Sagar is somehow able to travel to the \"far-off galaxy\" and find that Earth will prove no match for their forces, but he never reports back, so the invasion is called off.&nbsp; Why, you ask?&nbsp; Because of where he parked his ship.&nbsp; When he goes back to his ship to signal to Ba-Korr that the invasion should proceed as planned, he finds his ship gone -- he's trapped!\n\nThe story's final panel that reveals why the ship disappeared became the year's best laugh-out-loud moment.\n[ASSET: center | SlowSinkingQuicksand | width=67%]\n\nHe parked his space ship in a bog of \"slow-sinking quicksand.\"&nbsp; And to add to the humor, he concludes that, \"There is but one thing to do... I must <i>follow</i> my ship to the bottom of this bog -- to remain there for all eternity -- to pay for my fatal error!\"&nbsp; <i>OMG!&nbsp; </i>What a silly story, but one with such an ironic ending that I burst out laughing!&nbsp; <i>Hahahaha!</i>\n\nOnce again, these aliens from \"far-off\" galaxies somehow are able to detect Earth and want to conquer it, even though there must be countless planets in their <i>own</i> galaxies that would be much more convenient!&nbsp; But, perhaps not!&nbsp; I mean, if you're able to travel to Earth <i>and</i> back within a mere 48 hours, despite the distance, perhaps <i>any</i> planet is convenient!\n\n[ASSET: right | CreepyTinkerer | width=12%] However, I also want to give out an Honorable Mention for the face of the Tinkerer in the second story that made up [LINK:1963:ASM_2_asm1|ASM #2|2026-07-31].&nbsp; Steve Ditko strikes just the right look for a character who turns out to be every bit as creepy as this panel shows him to be.\n[BR]\n[BR] (Not only that, but -- you guessed it -- he's also an alien who's come to subjugate Earth!)\n\n<b>Best Credit Box</b>\n\nIt's probably not fair to grant an award based on a sample size of 1, but December's [LINK:1963:TOS_51_iron0|TOS #51|2027-04-16] was the first I'd seen of any \"playfulness\" in the credit box.&nbsp; <i>Hardly</i> worth more than a chuckle though, so I choose to hold off this award until things <i>really</i> get crazy!\n\n\n<b>Biggest Blunder</b>\n\nFrom 3-toed Hulks ([LINK:1963:AVEN_2|AVEN #2|2027-01-08]) to geography gaffes ([LINK:1963:TOS_51_iron0|TOS #51|2027-04-16]), there certainly <i>were</i> blunders aplenty this year, but did any of them elevate to being award-worthy contenders?&nbsp; Let's look over the list of ones that rose at least to <i>head-scratchers</i>:\n\n\n<b>FIXME</b>\n\n\n<b>Biggest Surprise of the Year</b>\n\nOne look at the incredibly bland cover of <i>Amazing Spider-Man</i> 10 ([LINK:1963:ASM_10|ASM #10|2027-04-05]) would lead one to expect greatness, which is why I found myself surprised by its outstanding quality, even in a month that did not even feature one of Spider-Man's mainline villains.\n\n\n<b>Spiral Award</b>\n\n[HANG0] [LINK:1963:JIM_91_thor0|JIM #91|2026-07-25] -- Sandu goes from \"Wow, look, I can levitate things!\" to \"I can rule the world!\" over the course of three pages, all while Loki pulls the strings from up in Asgard.&nbsp; (But <i>hey</i>, it's the Silver Age -- there's no time to <i>waste</i>!)\n\n[ASSET: right | MisterMxyzptlk | width=12%] [HANG0] [LINK:1963:JIM_95_thor0|JIM #95|2026-10-20] -- Professor Zaxton goes \"world's foremost theoretical physicist\" to maniacal would-be ruler of the world in only 8 pages, creating an evil duplicate of Thor in the process, not to mention entire office buildings, planes, and ultimately himself -- logic be damned!&nbsp; I kept waiting for Mister Mxyzptlk to appear -- it seemed right up his alley.&nbsp; <i>Argh!</i>\n\n[ASSET: left | BlobAcceleration | width=12%] [HANG0] [LINK:1963:XMEN_3|XMEN #3|2027-03-10] -- The Blob learns that he is a mutant, escapes from the X-Men's mansion, takes over the carnival where he was employed, and attacks the X-Men's headquarters over the course of only <b>five pages</b> (9-14) -- the very <i>definition</i> of plot acceleration!\n\n\n\n<b>Best and Worst Things to Happen to Marvel This Year</b>\n\nThe best thing for this year was the further establishment of the shared Marvel Universe.&nbsp; The crossovers that were only <i>hinted</i> at in late 1962 actually bore fruit this year...................\n\nBut I think it's fair to point out an Honorable Mention, which is the willingness near the end of the year to lean into the cliffhanger endings.&nbsp; This was shown mainly in later issues of <i>Journey Into Mystery</i> and <i>Tales to Astonish</i>.&nbsp; The sample size wasn't enough to push this across the finish line though.\n\nThe worst thing... <b>FIXME</b>\n(Stan Lee still being the only plotter?)\n\n\n<b>Trope of the Year Award</b>\n\nThere were two recurring finalists for the award this year.&nbsp; Let's break them down...\n\nThe selling of secrets to Communists (see [LINK:1963:TTA_50_giant0|TTA #50|2027-01-14] for my rundown, and note that it also appears in [LINK:1963:TTA_51_giant0|TTA #51|2027-02-09]).\n\nAnd the dreaded abandoned or deserted castle!"
}